\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We design experiments to test three predictions of the dual-mechanism analysis. First, does step-independent training collapse while episode-level training avoids collapse? Second, can we separate the individual contributions of information loss (M1) and sign misattribution (M2) through factorial manipulation? Third, how does window size $h$ modulate the severity of both mechanisms?

\subsection{Environment}
\label{sec:environment}

We evaluate on ALFWorld~\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment in which an agent interacts with simulated rooms through natural language commands. ALFWorld defines six task types (pick, clean, heat, cool, examine, pick two) that require sequential reasoning over multiple interaction steps. The agent issues text commands (e.g., \texttt{go to countertop 1}, \texttt{take knife 2 from drawer 3}) and receives textual observations of the environment state. Episodes terminate after task completion or a maximum of 50 steps. The reward is binary: $R \in \{0, r_+\}$, with $r_+$ awarded only upon successful task completion.

ALFWorld tasks exhibit strong sequential dependencies. A typical task requires the agent to locate an object (steps 1--5), pick it up (step 6), navigate to a target receptacle (steps 7--10), and place it (step 11). Under step-independent windowing with $h = 2$, the window containing the placement action has no access to the exploration steps that determined the target object. Context truncation therefore directly impairs the policy's ability to condition late actions on early observations.

\subsection{Base Model and Supervised Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:base_model}

We use Qwen3-1.7B (instruct variant) as the base policy. Before RL training, we perform supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on 2{,}546 expert demonstrations from ALFWorld for 2 epochs with learning rate $5 \times 10^{-6}$. The resulting SFT checkpoint achieves 12.5\% success rate on a held-out 32-task in-distribution validation set (4 of 32 tasks). The pre-SFT model achieves 0\% success in zero-shot evaluation, so SFT provides a necessary initialization for RL.

This low baseline is analytically significant. At success probability $p = 0.125$ with group size $K = 8$, Proposition~\ref{prop:correct_sign} predicts that approximately 80.9\% of gradient updates carry incorrect advantage sign under step-independent training. The SFT checkpoint therefore occupies the regime where sign misattribution is most severe.

\subsection{RL Training Configuration}
\label{sec:rl_config}

All conditions use the RAGEN framework~\cite{ragen2025} with the GRPO algorithm~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}. Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameters} lists the shared hyperparameters. The discount factor $\gamma$ applies only to step-independent return computation (Equation~\ref{eq:step_return}); episode-level conditions use undiscounted returns over the complete trajectory.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Shared RL training hyperparameters across all conditions.}
\label{tab:hyperparameters}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Parameter & Value \\
\midrule
Group size $K$ & 8 \\
Learning rate & $1 \times 10^{-6}$ \\
KL penalty $\beta$ & 0.01 \\
Discount $\gamma$ & 0.95 \\
Invalid action penalty & 0.1 \\
Tasks per training step & 16 \\
Training steps & 15 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:eval_protocol}

We evaluate on two task sets: (1)~a 32-task in-distribution set from the original RAGEN validation split, and (2)~a 134-task out-of-distribution (OOD) set for generalization assessment. All main results report the 134-task OOD set; in-distribution numbers serve as secondary references. Evaluation runs every 5 training steps.

The primary metric is success rate (pass@1): the fraction of episodes completed within 50 steps. We also report pass@$k$ for $k \in \{2, 4, 8\}$. As a diagnostic, we track \textit{action validity rate}, defined as the fraction of agent outputs that constitute syntactically valid environment commands (as opposed to malformed, empty, or repeated text). Action validity measures basic policy coherence, distinct from task completion. Results report single-seed or two-seed runs depending on availability; seed counts are noted per condition.

\subsection{Factorial Mechanism Separation}
\label{sec:factorial_experiments}

The dual-mechanism analysis (Section~\ref{sec:dual_mechanism}) predicts that information loss (M1) and sign misattribution (M2) produce qualitatively different training failures. To separate their effects, we implement the $2 \times 2$ factorial design described in Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design}, varying two factors: observation scope (truncated window vs.\ full trajectory) and advantage scope (per-window vs.\ episode-level).

\paragraph{Cell A: Truncated observation + step-level advantage.}
Window size $h{=}2$ with per-window advantage computation. Both mechanisms are active: the policy observes only 2-step windows (M1) and each window receives an advantage whose sign is determined by the episode outcome rather than local action quality (M2). This is the default RAGEN configuration and represents the community standard for multi-turn agent RL. Two seeds.

\paragraph{Cell B: Truncated observation + episode-level advantage.}
Window size $h{=}2$, but all windows within an episode share a single episode-level advantage instead of independent per-window values. M1 remains active (observations are still truncated to 2-step windows), while M2 is eliminated (all windows receive the same advantage sign, so no individual window can be misattributed). The comparison of Cell~A against Cell~B isolates the contribution of sign misattribution: any difference in training stability is attributable to M2 alone. Two seeds.

\paragraph{Cell D: Full observation + episode-level advantage.}
The complete trajectory is processed as a single sequence with one shared advantage. Neither mechanism is active: the full context eliminates information loss, and the single advantage eliminates sign misattribution. The comparison of Cell~B against Cell~D isolates the contribution of information loss: any performance gap reflects how truncated context limits the policy's ability to learn cross-step strategies. Two seeds.

The fourth cell (full observation + step-level advantage) is degenerate: with full context, only one window spans the entire episode, so per-window and episode-level advantage coincide. The factorial design therefore yields three distinct conditions that test qualitative predictions about training dynamics, not quantitative effect decomposition (Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design}).

\subsection{Additional Experimental Conditions}
\label{sec:additional_conditions}

\paragraph{Dense reward baseline.}
Step-independent $h{=}2$ with a per-step bonus of $+0.15$ for each syntactically valid action. This tests whether dense reward shaping can compensate for step-independent credit assignment failure. Training extends to 30 steps.

\paragraph{REINFORCE++ baseline.}
Episode-level training with a per-token running-mean baseline instead of GRPO group normalization. No value function is used.\footnote{This condition was originally planned as a GAE baseline with a learned value function. We substituted REINFORCE++ after TRL~1.1.0 removed the \texttt{AutoModelForCausalLMWithValueHead} class required for critic-based methods.}

\paragraph{Discount factor sweep.}
Step-independent $h{=}5$ with $\gamma \in \{0.50, 0.80, 0.95, 1.00\}$. This sweep isolates the role of magnitude decay in training collapse. At $\gamma = 1.0$, all windows receive identical return magnitude from the terminal reward; discount-induced magnitude variation vanishes entirely, but sign misattribution persists because each window still receives an independently normalized advantage. If collapse occurs at $\gamma = 1.0$, magnitude decay cannot be the primary cause.

All four $\gamma$ values produce complete training collapse: validation success drops to 0\% within 5 training steps across the sweep ($\gamma = 0.50$: collapsed at step~1; $\gamma = 0.80$: 3.1\% at step~5 then 0\%; $\gamma = 0.95$: 0\% at step~5; $\gamma = 1.0$: collapsed). The uniformity of collapse across $\gamma$ values, including $\gamma = 1.0$ where no magnitude decay exists, confirms that sign misattribution is the operative failure mechanism. Magnitude decay modulates the speed of collapse but is neither necessary nor sufficient to cause it.

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.}
Step-independent training with $h \in \{2, 5, 10, 20\}$ compared against episode-level training ($h = T$, the full trajectory). Smaller $h$ increases M1 severity through reduced per-window context and produces more windows per episode. OOD success rates are: $h{=}2$: 0\%, $h{=}5$: 0\%, $h{=}10$: 6.25\%, $h{=}20$: 15.6\%, episode-level: 23.88\%.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1
The transition between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ marks a cliff where sufficient context begins to offset information loss.

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Results are organized around three comparisons: (1)~episode-level vs.\ step-independent training dynamics (Section~\ref{sec:results_main}), (2)~factorial mechanism separation (Section~\ref{sec:results_factorial}), and (3)~window-size ablation (Section~\ref{sec:results_hsweep}).

\subsection{Cross-Scale Verification}
\label{sec:cross_scale}

The 1.7B experiments establish that step-independent training collapses while episode-level training succeeds. Both mechanisms could in principle be scale-dependent: a larger model might compensate for information loss through greater capacity, or resist sign misattribution through more stable optimization. We test scale invariance by replicating the key factorial conditions on Qwen3-8B.

\paragraph{Setup.}
The 8B SFT baseline uses the same training protocol (2{,}546 demonstrations, 2 epochs, learning rate $5 \times 10^{-6}$). It achieves 68.75\% in-distribution success ($T{=}0$, deterministic) and 80.6\% OOD success ($T{=}0.4$, stochastic sampling), far above the 1.7B baseline of 12.5\%.% exp_id: 8b_sft_baseline, 8b_det_eval_full, 7b_sft_eval_ood
This higher starting point provides a stronger test: improvement over an already-competent baseline would confirm that episode-level RL adds value beyond what scale alone provides.

\paragraph{Information loss.}
Restricting the 8B model's observation window to $h{=}2$ at inference time drops in-distribution success from 68.75\% to 31.25\%, a 37.5 percentage point gap.% exp_id: 8b_info_loss_eval
The relative magnitude is comparable to the 1.7B case. Larger models do not implicitly reconstruct missing context from a 2-step window.

\paragraph{Cell A collapse.}
Step-independent training on the 8B model collapses within two gradient steps: in-distribution success drops from 28.1\% (before training) to 12.5\% (step~1) to 1.5\% (step~2).% exp_id: 8b_cell_a_episode
The collapse trajectory matches the 1.7B pattern, consistent with the sign misattribution rate predicted by Proposition~\ref{prop:correct_sign}.

\paragraph{Cell D: episode-level training.}
Episode-level GRPO reaches 96.9\% in-distribution success at step~10 and 90.3\% OOD success (134 tasks, $T{=}0.4$), a 9.7 percentage point improvement over the SFT baseline on OOD tasks.% exp_id: 8b_cell_c_episode_v9d, 8b_v9d_ood_eval_v5
Training reward saturates to 100\% at step~12. The gain over an 80.6\% SFT baseline confirms that correct credit assignment enables RL to improve beyond supervised learning even from a strong initialization.

Table~\ref{tab:8b_results} summarizes the 8B conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{8B model results on ALFWorld. In-Dist: 32-task validation set; OOD: 134-task held-out test set. SFT in-distribution and SFT $h{=}2$ use deterministic decoding ($T{=}0$); all OOD results and RL evaluations use $T{=}0.4$. Cell~A reports the value at step~2 (post-collapse). Cell~D reports the best checkpoint (step~10).}
\label{tab:8b_results}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{In-Dist (\%)} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & 68.75 & 80.60 \\  % exp_id: 8b_det_eval_full (in-dist T=0), 7b_sft_eval_ood (OOD T=0.4)
SFT $h{=}2$ & 31.25 & --- \\  % exp_id: 8b_det_eval_h2 (T=0)
Cell A: step-indep.\ ($h{=}2$) & 28.1 $\to$ 1.5 & --- \\  % exp_id: 8b_cell_a_episode
Cell D: episode-level & \textbf{96.88} & \textbf{90.30} \\  % exp_id: 8b_cell_c_episode_v9d, 8b_v9d_ood_eval_v5
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Both the failure and the fix are scale-invariant. Step-independent training collapses within two steps at 8B, compared to 3--15 steps at 1.7B; episode-level training not only avoids collapse but exceeds the SFT ceiling by a substantial margin. Due to computational constraints of 8B-scale FSDP training, 8B results report single-seed runs.

